  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper is present. Table 1's extracted layout is flattened and difficult to align; Table 2 reports aggregate changes more clearly.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study discusses polysemanticity but tests parameter-level contributions to coding, not individual-neuron responses or any listed claim version.",
    "gate_note": "The paper identifies a Coding Spot by gradient-based parameter importance and zeros selected parameters, then reports coding and general-task benchmark changes. These results concern a parameter subset and task performance, not neurons responding to distinct inputs, so no listed version is tested. Table 2's parameter-ablation results are recorded as related, off-version findings; task-specific benchmark scores from Table 1 are left out because its extracted layout is difficult to align.",
    "test_reason": "The study ablates a gradient-selected subset of individual weights (not neurons) and reports benchmark changes. It calls the subset 'polysemantic' and defines a 'monosemanticity score', but it measures no neuron's responses to inputs. No version (neurons responding to unrelated inputs, their share, cause, or measurement) is tested. It cites Elhage et al. 2022 only as background.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors also claim, somewhat inconsistently, that ablation largely preserves non-coding abilities (abstract) while general tasks such as GSM8K drop sharply. They call the subset polysemantic at the parameter level, and say HellaSwag is less affected.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses gradient-based parameter-importance scoring across programming languages and zeros selected parameters; it does not use a neuron-level response or interpretation method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported aggregate changes vary with the deactivated fraction and model. The authors also describe different monosemanticity-score optima across models.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Zeroing 0.0025–0.25% of gradient-selected 'Coding Spot' parameters cuts HumanEval by 74–100% and the average general-task score by 26–55%, across three Llama-family models (Table 2).",
        "proposed_claim": "Parameter subsets important for one task (coding) also contribute to other tasks, a parameter-level 'polysemanticity' distinct from neuron polysemanticity.",
