    "peer_reviewed": "Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics",
    "problems": "The study reports parameter-level ablations and benchmark changes, not neuron-level response evidence. It reports no uncertainty estimates. The authors note that the Coding Spot threshold is selected empirically, parameter zeroing may affect results, and the study is limited to Llama-family models (while including CodeLlama).",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 1's individual benchmark values are not separately extracted because the provided flattened layout makes some model/metric alignments unclear; Table 2's aggregate general-task and coding changes are retained.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No vision models or image neurons."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No MLP-neuron activation analysis or per-layer share; the parameter subset is not neurons."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "Superposition is not tested."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Initialization, regularization and training noise are not studied."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No comparison of activation functions or architectures for neuron polysemanticity."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No reinterpretation of canonical neurons."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Activation ranges and explanation scoring are not varied. The paper's 'Ms' score is a task-performance ratio, not a neuron explanation score."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The rows were correctly left with versions ['none']. Their states are better read as 'cannot_determine' for the claim than as effects of the ablation. Table 2's Ms values are a task-impact ratio the authors call 'monosemanticity'; this label should not be confused with neuron monosemanticity. The study's TruthfulQA and WinoGrande scores rise at higher ablation fractions; those values sit in Table 1 and were left out by the reader."
  },
